<component>
  <section>
    <templateId root="2.16.840.1.113883.10.20.22.2.9" extension="2014-06-09"/>
    <code code="51847-2" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="Evaluation + Plan note"/>
    <title>Assessment and Plan</title>
    <text><list listType="ordered"><item><content styleCode="Bold">Prostate cancer, unfavorable intermediate risk, after radiotherapy with androgen deprivation (C61)</content>. PSA 0.4 ng/mL six weeks after radiotherapy and three weeks after the end of androgen deprivation. The PSA nadir is usually reached over one to two years; a small temporary rise (a bounce) can occur and is not recurrence. Recurrence after radiotherapy is defined as a PSA rise of 2 ng/mL above the nadir (Phoenix). [S]<list><item>PSA every six months for five years, then yearly.</item><item>Written survivorship care plan given today, with the surveillance schedule and symptoms to report.</item></list></item><item><content styleCode="Bold">After external beam radiotherapy (Z92.3)</content>. Grade 1 urinary and bowel effects of radiotherapy, both improving. IPSS 12, up from 7 before treatment. [S]<list><item>Continue tamsulosin 0.4 mg in the evening; review at the next visit, with a view to stopping it once symptoms settle.</item><item>Report blood in the urine or rectal bleeding at any time, which would need urology or gastroenterology assessment.</item></list></item><item><content styleCode="Bold">Androgen deprivation therapy: completed (Z79.899)</content>. Testosterone 38 ng/dL, still low; it recovers slowly over months at his age, and hot flashes, fatigue, and weight should improve as it does. [S]<list><item>Testosterone with the next PSA.</item><item>Lipid panel and A1c at one year; DXA again in one to two years.</item><item>Continue cholecalciferol 1000 units daily and resistance exercise.</item></list></item></list><paragraph>Protocol monitoring: items the monitoring protocol calls for at this visit. [S]</paragraph><table><thead><tr><th>Domain</th><th>Measure</th><th>Interval</th><th>Status</th><th>Detail</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Laboratory</td><td>PSA after radiotherapy</td><td>Every 6 to 12 months for 5 years, then yearly</td><td>Completed today</td><td>0.4 ng/mL, first value after radiotherapy; next in six months</td></tr><tr><td>Laboratory</td><td>Testosterone recovery</td><td>With PSA after androgen deprivation ends</td><td>Completed today</td><td>38 ng/dL, not yet recovered</td></tr><tr><td>Laboratory</td><td>Hemoglobin A1c or fasting glucose, and lipid panel</td><td>Yearly after androgen deprivation</td><td>Due</td><td>Due in four months, one year after baseline</td></tr><tr><td>Imaging</td><td>Bone density (DXA)</td><td>Every 1 to 2 years after androgen deprivation</td><td>Up to date</td><td>Baseline six months ago; next in one to two years</td></tr><tr><td>Examination</td><td>Digital rectal examination</td><td>Yearly after curative treatment</td><td>Due</td><td>Not useful this soon after radiotherapy; due in one year</td></tr><tr><td>Examination</td><td>Blood pressure and cardiovascular assessment</td><td>Every visit while on androgen deprivation and after</td><td>Completed today</td><td>128/74 mm Hg seated; no dizziness on standing on tamsulosin</td></tr><tr><td>Survey</td><td>Urinary symptoms (IPSS)</td><td>Every follow-up visit, particularly the first two years</td><td>Completed today</td><td>IPSS 12, up from 7 before treatment; improving on tamsulosin</td></tr><tr><td>Survey</td><td>Bowel function after radiotherapy</td><td>Every visit after radiotherapy</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Softer stools once or twice a day with mild urgency, settling; no bleeding</td></tr><tr><td>Survey</td><td>Bowel baseline missed before radiotherapy</td><td>Baseline before radiotherapy</td><td>Deferred</td><td>Not asked at the visit before radiotherapy, a deviation from the protocol. The wellness visit five weeks before that had recorded regular, formed stools once a day, and he confirms this today, so a baseline was available and no harm resulted. The bowel question is now on the pre-radiotherapy checklist</td></tr><tr><td>Survey</td><td>Sexual function</td><td>Periodically after treatment</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Reviewed; expected recovery after androgen deprivation explained; no treatment requested</td></tr><tr><td>Medication safety</td><td>Hot flashes and fatigue</td><td>Every visit until androgen deprivation effects resolve</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Fewer hot flashes; fatigue from radiotherapy improving</td></tr><tr><td>Survey</td><td>Distress and depression screen (PHQ-2)</td><td>At intervals</td><td>Completed today</td><td>PHQ-2 score 0</td></tr><tr><td>Survey</td><td>Cognition</td><td>Periodically after androgen deprivation</td><td>Up to date</td><td>Mini-Cog 5 of 5 at the wellness visit; no change reported</td></tr><tr><td>Self-management</td><td>Survivorship care plan and lifestyle</td><td>After treatment; lifestyle every visit</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Written plan given; walking resumed; no tobacco</td></tr></tbody></table><paragraph>Goal progress:</paragraph><list><item>Reach an informed, shared decision on treatment after hearing every reasonable option, including observation. Outcome by 2026-04-14: Radiotherapy with six months of androgen deprivation chosen at the radiation oncology consultation [S]</item><item>Complete six months of androgen deprivation and the course of radiotherapy with side effects managed. Outcome by 2026-10-27: Two leuprolide depots and 37 fractions completed; testosterone castrate on treatment; mild hot flashes and urinary frequency, managed [S]</item><item>Protect bone health on androgen deprivation: baseline DXA, vitamin D of 30 ng/mL or more, dietary calcium, and weight-bearing exercise. Outcome by 2026-07-14: Femoral neck T-score -1.4; vitamin D 34 ng/mL on supplementation; walking daily [S]</item><item>PSA falling after radiotherapy, with a written surveillance plan. Outcome by 2026-10-27: PSA 0.4 ng/mL six weeks after radiotherapy; PSA every six months planned [S]</item></list><paragraph>Immunizations given today: High-dose influenza vaccine, this season [S]</paragraph><paragraph>Guideline basis: NCCN Clinical Practice Guidelines in Oncology: Prostate Cancer (2026); Eastham JA, et al. Clinically Localized Prostate Cancer: AUA/ASTRO Guideline, Part III: Principles of Radiation and Future Directions. J Urol 2022;208(1):26-33 (2022); Skolarus TA, et al. American Cancer Society prostate cancer survivorship care guidelines. CA Cancer J Clin 2014;64(4):225-249 (2014); CMID: Clinical Monitoring Intelligence Database, prostate cancer monograph (2026)</paragraph><paragraph>Follow-up: Primary care in three months to review tamsulosin and urinary and bowel symptoms. PSA and testosterone in six months. Radiation oncology review in six weeks, three months after treatment. Influenza vaccine given today. [S]</paragraph></text>

